{% comment %}
  Orlant additions to the product card.

  Rendered from inside snippets/card-product.liquid rather than replacing it.
  Dawn's card already handles hover-second-image, sale and sold-out badges,
  compare-at pricing, swatches and quick-add across 600+ lines; forking that to
  add two fields would mean maintaining a parallel copy and re-fixing every
  upstream bug twice.

  Accepts:
  - card_product: {Object} Required.
  - part: {String} Which piece to render:
      "tagline" — one-line spec under the product title (orlant.card_tagline)
      "badge"   — merchandising badge (orlant.badge), e.g. "New", "Best seller"
  - section_id: {String} Required for "badge", to keep ids unique.

  Both render nothing when their metafield is empty.

  Usage:
  {% render 'orlant-product-card', card_product: card_product, part: 'tagline' %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- case part -%}
  {%- when 'tagline' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Rendered from both of Dawn's card__information blocks. Dawn emits two —
      one inside .card__inner and one below — and picks between them in CSS
      depending on card style and whether the product has media, exactly as it
      does with the product title. Rendering into only one would drop the
      tagline for some style/media combinations, so this mirrors Dawn and
      accepts the same hidden duplicate Dawn already ships for the title.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign tagline = card_product.metafields.orlant.card_tagline.value -%}
    {%- if tagline != blank -%}
      <p class="orlant-card__tagline">{{ tagline }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

  {%- when 'badge' -%}
    {%- assign badge = card_product.metafields.orlant.badge.value -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Only shown when the product is in stock and not on sale — Dawn already
      renders "Sold out" and "Sale" badges in this same corner, and stacking a
      third label there turns the card into a sticker wall.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if badge != blank and card_product.available -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Written as `unless compare_at > price` rather than `if compare_at <=
        price`: compare_at_price is nil on anything not on sale, and nil <= n
        is false in Liquid, so the positive form suppressed the badge on
        exactly the products meant to show it. This is Dawn's own idiom.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- unless card_product.compare_at_price > card_product.price -%}
        <span
          id="OrlantBadge-{{ section_id }}-{{ card_product.id }}"
          class="badge orlant-card__badge"
        >
          {{- badge -}}
        </span>
      {%- endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}
{%- endcase -%}
